{"pack": "m1learn", "voice": "boy", "speaker": {"am_puck": 0.8, "af_heart": 0.2}, "audio": "m1learn.mp4", "model": "Kokoro-82M v1.0 (Apache 2.0) via kokoro-onnx (MIT); misaki phonemizer (Apache 2.0)", "gap": 0.45, "clips": {"Hmm, not quite.": [0.0, 1.193], "Almost! Let\u2019s look again.": [1.643, 1.527], "Good try!": [3.619, 0.982], "Hmm, not that one.": [5.051, 1.267], "Let me show you!": [6.768, 1.194], "Adding means putting groups together to find how many in all.": [8.412, 3.179], "Here are 8 fish and 5 fish. How many in all?": [12.041, 3.031], "Here is a sailor's trick: make a ten!": [15.521, 2.148], "8 needs 2 more to make 10. So take 2 fish from the group of 5.": [18.119, 4.413], "Now we have 10 and 3 more. 10 + 3 = 13. So 8 + 5 = 13!": [22.982, 6.785], "9 crabs and 4 crabs. How many crabs in all?": [30.217, 3.277], "9 + 1 = 10, and 3 more makes 13. So 9 + 4 = 13.": [33.944, 5.874], "9 needs 1 more to make 10.": [40.268, 1.848], "Take 1 from the 4. Now you have 10 and 3 more.": [42.566, 2.94], "12 is one too few. 9 needs 1 more to make 10. Then add what is left of the 4.": [45.956, 6.104], "14 is one too many. Make 10 first: 9 + 1. Then count what is left of the 4.": [52.51, 6.403], "12, 13, or 14?": [59.363, 1.731], "7 + 3 = 10, and 3 more makes 13. So 7 + 6 = 13.": [61.544, 6.12], "7 needs 3 more to make 10.": [68.114, 1.977], "Take 3 from the 6. Now you have 10 and 3 more.": [70.541, 3.329], "11 is too few. 7 + 3 makes 10. How many are left of the 6?": [74.321, 4.999], "12 is one too few. 7 + 3 makes 10, and 6 has 3 more left.": [79.769, 5.343], "11, 13, or 12?": [85.562, 1.693], "You counted the whole catch: 13 fish! Thank you, {name}!": [87.704, 2.832], "Jonah has 6 fish and catches 8 more. How many fish in all?": [90.986, 3.736], "8 + 2 = 10, and 4 more makes 14.": [95.172, 3.205], "8 needs 2 more to make 10.": [98.827, 1.763], "Take 2 from the 6. Now you have 10 and 4 more.": [101.04, 3.201], "13 is one too few. 8 + 2 makes 10. How many are left of the 6?": [104.691, 5.443], "15 is one too many. Make 10 first: 8 + 2. Then add the rest of the 6.": [110.584, 5.798], "13, 14, or 15?": [116.832, 1.764], "A ruler tells us how long something is.": [119.046, 2.207], "Each space between two numbers is one inch. Always start at the 0!": [121.704, 4.397], "Before I build the real bridge, I make a small model. Here is a model plank.": [126.55, 4.727], "I put one end of the plank on the 0. The other end is at the 5.": [131.727, 4.105], "So the plank is 5 inches long!": [136.282, 1.945], "How long is this plank?": [138.677, 1.493], "The plank starts at 0 and ends at 3. It is 3 inches long.": [140.62, 4.159], "Look where the plank ends.": [145.229, 1.425], "The plank starts at 0. Count each inch to its end.": [147.104, 3.308], "The plank goes past the 2. Look where the plank ends.": [150.862, 3.184], "The plank does not reach the 4. Look where it ends.": [154.496, 3.059], "2 inches, 3 inches, or 4 inches?": [158.005, 2.357], "It starts at 0 and ends at 6, so it is 6 inches long.": [160.812, 3.876], "Find the number right under the end of the plank.": [165.138, 2.499], "The end of the plank is at the 6.": [168.087, 1.873], "The plank does not reach the 7. Look where it ends.": [170.411, 3.273], "The plank goes past the 5. Look where it ends.": [174.133, 2.863], "6 inches, 7 inches, or 5 inches?": [177.446, 2.624], "I know how long each board must be. I cut planks to match my model. Take them to Penny at the broken bridge!": [180.52, 6.986], "It starts at 0 and ends at 4, so it is 4 inches long.": [187.956, 3.757], "Start at the 0.": [192.163, 1.238], "Count the inches to the end of the plank.": [193.851, 2.147], "The plank goes past the 3. Look where it ends.": [196.447, 2.884], "The plank does not reach the 5. Look where it ends.": [199.781, 3.115], "4 inches, 3 inches, or 5 inches?": [203.346, 2.399], "Shapes have straight sides and corners.": [206.195, 2.029], "Triangle: 3 sides. Square, rectangle: 4. Pentagon: 5. Hexagon: 6!": [208.674, 5.854], "Let's count the sides of this shape together.": [214.978, 2.212], "1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. It has 6 sides.": [217.64, 3.504], "A shape with 6 sides is a hexagon!": [221.594, 2.155], "How many sides does this shape have?": [224.199, 1.945], "It has 3 sides. A shape with 3 sides is a triangle.": [226.593, 3.761], "Touch each side with your finger as you count.": [230.804, 2.243], "Count: 1, 2, \u2026": [233.498, 1.82], "Count again, one side at a time. Touch each side only once.": [235.768, 3.677], "5 is too many. Count each side once with your finger.": [239.894, 3.544], "3, 4, or 5?": [243.888, 1.33], "What is this shape called?": [245.668, 1.419], "It has 5 sides, so it is a pentagon.": [247.537, 2.341], "Count its sides first.": [250.328, 1.48], "It has 5 sides. A shape with 5 sides is a\u2026": [252.258, 3.308], "A hexagon has 6 sides. Count the sides of this shape.": [256.016, 3.652], "A square has 4 sides. Count the sides of this shape.": [260.118, 3.317], "hexagon, pentagon, or square?": [263.885, 1.989], "You know your shapes! Now let's build the bridge. Each piece of the plan needs the right shape.": [266.324, 5.012], "How many sides does a hexagon have?": [271.786, 2.188], "A hexagon has 6 sides.": [274.424, 1.794], "Count the sides of the shape.": [276.668, 1.525], "Hex- shapes have more than 5 sides.": [278.643, 1.897], "A shape with 5 sides is a pentagon. A hexagon has more.": [280.99, 3.98], "8 is too many. Count the sides of the hexagon in the picture.": [285.42, 3.878], "5, 6, or 8?": [289.748, 1.345], "When we read, we can ask: WHO is in it? WHERE does it happen? WHEN? WHY? The answers hide in the words!": [291.543, 6.189], "A sentence is a group of words that tells one idea. It starts with a big letter and ends with a dot.": [298.181, 6.342], "Let's read Keeper Ada's logbook together.": [304.973, 2.468], "Who climbed the stairs? I look in the story\u2026 \"Keeper Ada climbed the tall stairs.\"": [307.891, 5.173], "So the answer is Keeper Ada! I found it in the words.": [313.514, 3.264], "WHEN did the lamp go out?": [317.228, 1.504], "The story starts: \"It was the night of the big storm.\" Then the lamp went out.": [319.182, 4.393], "Read the first words of the story again.": [324.025, 2.133], "The story starts with \"It was the night of\u2026\"": [326.608, 2.719], "The morning came later in the story. Read the first words: when did the lamp go out?": [329.777, 5.053], "There is no afternoon in the story. Read the first words again.": [335.28, 3.733], "On the night of the big storm, In the morning, after the storm, or On a rainy afternoon?": [339.463, 4.949], "WHY did Ada want the light to shine?": [344.861, 1.935], "Ada said, \"The light must shine for the ships.\" Ships need the light to stay safe.": [347.246, 4.695], "Find what Ada said.": [352.391, 1.291], "Ada said, \"The light must shine for the\u2026\"": [354.132, 2.412], "Ada did not need the light to read. Who needs a lighthouse at night?": [356.994, 3.862], "A lighthouse is not for cooking. Who sails at night and needs to see?": [361.306, 4.053], "For the ships, To read a book, or To cook dinner?": [365.808, 2.481], "The storm put the lamp out. Let's help Keeper Ada light it again!": [368.739, 3.868], "Read: \"Mo fixed the boat at the harbor.\" WHERE did Mo fix the boat?": [373.057, 3.751], "The sentence says \"at the harbor.\"": [377.258, 1.838], "WHERE asks about a place.": [379.546, 1.499], "Find the place word in the sentence: \"\u2026at the ___.\"": [381.496, 3.267], "The sentence does not say forest. Find the WHERE words: \"at the \u2026\".": [385.212, 4.596], "The sentence does not say farm. Read it again and find the place.": [390.258, 3.935], "At the harbor, In the forest, or At the farm?": [394.643, 2.385], "Some words name a whole group.": [397.478, 1.653], "A group of fish is called a school. A group of birds is called a flock.": [399.582, 4.198], "Look at all these cows standing together.": [404.23, 2.095], "A group of cows is called a herd.": [406.775, 1.838], "So I write: \"The herd is eating grass.\"": [409.063, 2.248], "This letter got wet in the storm. Which word fits? \"A ___ of fish swam by the pier.\"": [411.761, 5.196], "A group of fish is a school. \"A school of fish swam by the pier.\"": [417.407, 3.921], "Remember: what do we call a group of fish?": [421.778, 2.317], "A fish group has the same name as the place kids learn.": [424.546, 2.923], "A flock is a group of birds. These are fish.": [427.918, 2.799], "A herd is a group of cows. These are fish.": [431.167, 2.673], "school, flock, or herd?": [434.29, 1.483], "Which word fits? \"A ___ of birds flew over the lighthouse.\"": [436.223, 3.035], "A group of birds is a flock. \"A flock of birds flew over the lighthouse.\"": [439.708, 4.329], "Think about a group of birds.": [444.487, 1.632], "Birds fly together in a\u2026": [446.569, 2.015], "A herd is a group of cows. These are birds.": [449.034, 2.833], "A school is a group of fish. These are birds.": [452.317, 2.909], "herd, flock, or school?": [455.676, 1.473], "Perfect! The letters make sense again. Now I can deliver them all over the island. Thank you, {name}!": [457.599, 6.167], "Which word fits? \"The farmer's ___ of cows is in the field.\"": [464.216, 3.331], "A group of cows is a herd.": [467.997, 1.592], "What do we call a group of cows?": [470.039, 1.72], "It is not a school (fish) or a flock (birds).": [472.209, 2.511], "A flock is a group of birds, not cows.": [475.169, 2.176], "A school is a group of fish, not cows.": [477.795, 2.122], "Sometimes we read a word we don't know. Don't worry! The other words in the sentence can give us clues.": [480.367, 6.431], "Read: \"The storm made the sky dark and gloomy.\"": [487.249, 2.641], "Do I know \"gloomy\"? Hmm. The clue word \"dark\" is right next to it.": [490.34, 3.739], "So gloomy means dark and sad-looking!": [494.529, 2.112], "\"The sailor was so weary after the long trip that he fell asleep.\" What does weary mean?": [497.091, 5.203], "He fell asleep after a long trip, so weary means very tired.": [502.744, 3.781], "What did the sailor do at the end?": [506.975, 1.71], "He fell asleep after a long trip. So he was\u2026": [509.135, 3.036], "Being happy does not make you fall asleep. Read the clue again.": [512.621, 3.653], "Being fast does not make you fall asleep. What makes you sleepy?": [516.724, 3.921], "very tired, very happy, or very fast?": [521.095, 2.489], "\"The lighthouse beam swept across the dark sea.\" What is a beam?": [524.034, 3.784], "A lighthouse makes light, so a beam is a line of light.": [528.268, 3.006], "What does a lighthouse make at night?": [531.724, 1.773], "A lighthouse makes light. The beam goes across the dark sea.": [533.947, 3.608], "A lighthouse does not send fish across the sea. What does it make?": [538.005, 3.837], "You cannot see a sound in the dark. The clue is the dark sea.": [542.291, 3.578], "a kind of fish, a line of light, or a loud, deep sound?": [546.319, 3.302], "Great clue hunting! The storm washed the words off my signs. Now I can paint them again, nice and clear.": [550.071, 6.331], "\"The bread was so enormous that it did not fit in the basket.\" What does enormous mean?": [556.852, 5.163], "It did not fit in the basket, so enormous means very big.": [562.465, 3.607], "Find the clue: it did not fit in the basket.": [566.522, 2.501], "Things that do not fit are usually too\u2026": [569.473, 2.446], "A small loaf would fit in the basket. This one did not fit.": [572.369, 3.749], "Cold bread can still fit in a basket. Why did it not fit?": [576.567, 3.749], "very big, very small, or very cold?": [580.766, 2.233], "Our world has land and water. Land: a beach, a hill, an island. Water: an ocean, a river, a lake, a pond.": [583.449, 7.799], "Let's look at a model of our island. A model is a small picture of a real place.": [591.698, 4.963], "See? There is water all around the land. Land with water all around it is an island.": [597.111, 5.133], "The big salty water around it is the ocean!": [602.694, 2.432], "Water that flows across the land in a long line is a\u2026": [605.576, 3.153], "A river is water that flows across the land.": [609.179, 2.517], "This water moves. It flows from one place to another.": [612.146, 3.356], "On our island it runs between the village and the harbor.": [615.952, 3.068], "A pond is small and still. This water moves in a long line.": [619.47, 3.705], "An ocean is huge and salty, all around the land. This water flows across the land.": [623.626, 5.56], "pond, river, or ocean?": [629.636, 1.497], "A small body of still water on land is a\u2026": [631.582, 2.684], "A pond is a small body of still water.": [634.716, 2.266], "It is small, and the water stays still.": [637.432, 2.117], "Frogs and lily pads live in one in our forest.": [639.999, 2.773], "An ocean is huge, not small. This water is small and still.": [643.222, 3.689], "A river flows and moves. This water is still.": [647.361, 3.021], "pond, ocean, or river?": [650.831, 1.475], "You read our island like a scientist: ocean, river, pond and beaches!": [652.757, 4.117], "Land with water all around it is an\u2026": [657.324, 2.301], "An island is land with water all around it.": [660.075, 2.477], "Think about our home!": [663.001, 1.312], "It is land, not water.": [664.763, 1.495], "An ocean is water, not land. Look for land with water all around it.": [666.709, 4.31], "A river is water that flows. The answer is a kind of land.": [671.469, 3.778], "island, ocean, or river?": [675.697, 1.549], "A habitat is a home for plants and animals. Ponds, forests and beaches are habitats. Each has its own life.": [677.696, 7.553], "Let's observe this pond. I look closely and count the different living things.": [685.699, 4.552], "I see lily pads, reeds, a frog, a fish and a dragonfly. That is 5 kinds!": [690.701, 5.21], "Many kinds of living things share one habitat.": [696.361, 2.767], "Look at this forest habitat. Which group lives here?": [699.578, 3.051], "In the forest we see trees, birds and butterflies.": [703.079, 2.994], "Look at the picture. What do you see?": [706.523, 1.861], "A forest has lots of trees\u2026": [708.833, 1.818], "Crabs and sea stars live by the sea. This picture is a forest.": [711.101, 4.169], "Fish and seaweed live in water. This picture is a forest.": [715.721, 3.557], "trees, birds and butterflies, crabs, sea stars and sea snails, or fish and seaweed?": [719.727, 5.328], "Which habitat has MORE kinds of living things?": [725.505, 2.549], "Habitat A has 5 kinds of living things. Habitat B has only 1 kind.": [728.504, 5.247], "Count the different living things in each picture.": [734.201, 2.481], "One picture has 5 kinds. How many does the other have?": [737.131, 3.602], "Habitat B has fewer kinds. Count the living things in each picture.": [741.183, 4.317], "Habitat A, or Habitat B?": [745.951, 1.849], "Go observe! Frogs by the pond, crabs on the beach, robins in the meadow. Every habitat is full of life!": [748.25, 6.541], "A habitat is\u2026": [755.241, 1.375], "A habitat is the home where plants and animals live.": [757.066, 3.121], "A pond and a forest are habitats.": [760.637, 1.943], "Who lives in a pond or a forest?": [763.03, 1.779], "Animals live in a habitat, but a habitat is not an animal.": [765.259, 3.705], "A habitat is full of life. Think of the pond: who lives there?": [769.415, 3.835], "a home for plants and animals, a kind of animal that lives in ponds, or a place where nothing lives?": [773.7, 6.027], "Things are made of materials: wood, stone, rope, glass.": [780.177, 3.41], "Observe and sort them: hard or soft? Stiff or bendy?": [784.037, 3.471], "Let me test this rope. I bend it\u2026 it bends easily!": [787.958, 3.783], "I can even tie it in a knot. Something that bends easily is flexible.": [792.191, 4.228], "So rope is flexible!": [796.869, 1.473], "Which material is hard?": [798.792, 1.495], "Stone is hard. Wool and rope are soft and bendy.": [800.737, 2.845], "Hard things do not squish when you press them.": [804.032, 2.341], "Think of a rock on the beach.": [806.823, 1.514], "Wool is soft and squishy. Which one does not squish?": [808.787, 3.221], "Rope bends and is soft. Which one is hard?": [812.458, 2.815], "wool, stone, or rope?": [815.723, 1.396], "We need to tie the bridge pieces together. Which material is flexible enough to tie?": [817.569, 5.014], "Rope is flexible, so we can tie knots with it.": [823.033, 2.673], "To tie something, it must bend.": [826.156, 1.794], "Which one can make a knot?": [828.4, 1.47], "Glass is stiff and can break. It cannot bend into a knot.": [830.32, 3.585], "Stone is hard. It cannot bend into a knot.": [834.355, 2.389], "glass, stone, or rope?": [837.194, 1.487], "Here is strong, flexible rope for the bridge! Take it to Penny. Hard wood and bendy rope make a great team.": [839.132, 6.904], "Which one is flexible (bendy)?": [846.485, 1.891], "A rope bends easily, so it is flexible.": [848.826, 2.458], "Flexible means it bends easily.": [851.734, 1.999], "Which one could you tie in a knot?": [854.183, 1.707], "A rock is hard. It does not bend.": [856.34, 2.019], "A glass jar is stiff. It breaks if you bend it.": [858.809, 2.745], "a rope, a rock, or a glass jar?": [862.004, 1.828], "A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. It is how a bird sees it, flying high in the sky.": [864.282, 6.028], "On a map, small pictures called symbols show where things are.": [870.76, 3.314], "This tree symbol shows a forest. This blue line shows a river.": [874.524, 3.909], "The key tells us what each symbol means.": [878.883, 2.101], "On this map, what does the blue line show?": [881.434, 2.259], "The key says the blue line is a river.": [884.143, 2.084], "Look at the key next to the map.": [886.677, 1.717], "Blue is the color of water.": [888.845, 1.615], "Roads are not blue on this map. Look at the key: what does blue show?": [890.909, 3.991], "A fence is not blue on this map. Look at the key to see what blue means.": [895.35, 4.319], "a road, a river, or a fence?": [900.119, 1.71], "Look at the Atlas page. What is right next to the lighthouse?": [902.279, 3.381], "The lighthouse stands on a cliff right next to the sea.": [906.11, 2.737], "Find the lighthouse symbol on the map.": [909.297, 1.922], "What color is all around the lighthouse's cliff?": [911.669, 2.415], "The farm is far up the island, not next to the lighthouse.": [914.534, 3.111], "The forest is far away on the other side. Look right next to the lighthouse.": [918.095, 4.492], "the sea, the farm, or the forest?": [923.037, 1.768], "Now you can read the Atlas page! But first: explorer training!": [925.255, 3.997], "A map shows a place as if we look at it from\u2026": [929.701, 2.642], "A map shows a place seen from above.": [932.793, 1.949], "Think of a bird flying high.": [935.192, 1.613], "The bird looks down from\u2026": [937.255, 1.837], "From the side we see a picture, like a photo. A map shows a place the way a flying bird sees it.": [939.542, 5.971], "From the front we see the front of a house. A map shows a place the way a flying bird sees it.": [945.963, 5.815], "above, the side, or the front?": [952.229, 1.655], "We can make our own map! We draw the things in a place. We put each one where it really is.": [954.333, 5.277], "The storm washed my board map. Let's draw it again.": [960.06, 3.225], "The workshop is right next to the bakery in the village.": [963.735, 2.886], "So on the map, I draw the workshop next to the bakery!": [967.071, 3.015], "The farm is at the top of the island, far from the sea. Where does it go on our map?": [970.537, 4.687], "Spot A is at the top of the map, where the farm really is.": [975.673, 3.205], "The farm is at the TOP of the island.": [979.328, 1.966], "Find the spot at the top of the map.": [981.744, 1.891], "Spot B is at the bottom, by the sea. The farm is at the top.": [984.085, 3.824], "Spot C is not at the top. Find the spot at the top, far from the sea.": [988.359, 4.423], "spot A, spot B, or spot C?": [993.231, 1.892], "The beach is at the bottom of the island, by the sea. Where does the beach go?": [995.573, 4.212], "Spot B is at the bottom, next to the sea. That is where the beach really is.": [1000.235, 4.493], "The beach is at the BOTTOM, by the water.": [1005.178, 2.043], "Find the spot next to the blue sea at the bottom.": [1007.671, 2.543], "Spot A is at the top. The beach is at the bottom, by the sea.": [1010.664, 3.623], "Spot C is not at the bottom by the sea. Look at the bottom of the map.": [1014.738, 4.179], "Our new map is ready! Now nobody gets lost. Great mapmaking, {name}!": [1019.367, 3.962], "When we make a map, we put each place\u2026": [1023.779, 2.245], "On a map, each place goes where it really is. So the map helps us find our way.": [1026.474, 4.883], "A map must help people find places.": [1031.808, 2.143], "The bakery is next to the workshop. On the map, it is\u2026": [1034.401, 3.674], "If we put places anywhere, the map would not help anyone find them.": [1038.525, 3.934], "A map is not a list of names. Each place goes in its real spot.": [1042.909, 4.199], "where it really is, anywhere we like, or in ABC order by name?": [1047.558, 3.801], "Natural things come from nature: rivers, trees, beaches. People make other things: houses, roads, bridges.": [1051.809, 7.187], "Let's look through my telescope at our island.": [1059.446, 2.425], "I see the forest. People did not build it, so it is natural!": [1062.321, 3.801], "I see the pier. People built it with wood, so it is made by people.": [1066.573, 3.819], "Is the river natural or made by people?": [1070.841, 2.341], "The river comes from nature, so it is natural.": [1073.633, 2.727], "Did people build the river?": [1076.809, 1.561], "Rivers come from rain and springs in nature.": [1078.82, 2.567], "People did not make the river. It comes from nature.": [1081.837, 3.203], "natural, or made by people?": [1085.49, 1.699], "Which one was made by people?": [1087.638, 1.655], "People built the lighthouse, so it is made by people.": [1089.743, 2.895], "Which one was built with bricks or wood?": [1093.088, 2.02], "Nature did not build towers.": [1095.558, 1.771], "People did not make the ocean. It comes from nature.": [1097.779, 3.265], "Trees grow on their own. Which one did people build?": [1101.494, 3.225], "the ocean, the lighthouse, or the forest?": [1105.17, 2.025], "A good map shows natural places and places people made. Great eyes, {name}!": [1107.645, 4.403], "Which one is natural?": [1112.498, 1.411], "A beach is natural. Roads and houses are made by people.": [1114.36, 3.535], "Natural things come from nature.": [1118.345, 1.945], "People did not build it with tools.": [1120.74, 1.875], "People build roads. Look for the one that comes from nature.": [1123.064, 3.449], "People build houses. Look for the one that comes from nature.": [1126.963, 3.504], "a beach, a road, or a house?": [1130.917, 1.499], "Real history! Before electricity, lighthouses burned oil lamps. Keepers carried oil up and trimmed the wicks.": [1132.866, 6.96], "To compare is to look at two things. How are they the same? How are they different?": [1140.276, 4.551], "Now let's compare. Long ago: an oil flame, and a keeper climbing the stairs every day.": [1145.277, 5.527], "Today: real lighthouses use electric lights. Workers still check them, but nobody carries oil up anymore.": [1151.254, 6.601], "Long ago and today: same job, different ways!": [1158.305, 2.943], "Which light did lighthouses use LONG AGO?": [1161.698, 2.287], "Long ago, before electricity, lighthouses burned oil in lamps.": [1164.436, 3.883], "Long ago there was no electricity.": [1168.768, 2.041], "Keepers carried oil up the stairs for it.": [1171.26, 2.253], "Long ago there was no electricity, so there were no light bulbs.": [1173.963, 3.616], "an oil lamp, or an electric light bulb?": [1178.029, 2.333], "Long ago, what did keepers carry up the lighthouse stairs?": [1180.812, 3.093], "Keepers carried fuel, like oil, up the stairs for the lamp.": [1184.356, 3.488], "What did the flame need to keep burning?": [1188.294, 1.987], "Long ago there was no electricity: no light bulbs, no batteries.": [1190.731, 3.931], "Long ago there were no light bulbs. The flame needed something to burn.": [1195.112, 4.188], "Long ago lights did not use batteries. The flame needed something to burn.": [1199.75, 4.619], "new light bulbs, fuel for the lamp, or batteries for the light?": [1204.819, 3.499], "Same job, different ways! In our story, my lighthouse guides the Atlas pages home.": [1208.768, 5.293], "Today, most real lighthouse lights are\u2026": [1214.511, 2.301], "Today real lighthouses use electric lights.": [1217.262, 2.547], "Long ago they used oil. What do we use today?": [1220.259, 2.819], "The same thing that lights your house at night.": [1223.528, 2.188], "Candles are too weak to be seen far out at sea. Think of lights in houses today.": [1226.166, 4.9], "A campfire on top of a tall tower? The wind would blow it out. Think of today.": [1231.516, 4.792], "electric, candles, or campfires?": [1236.758, 2.047], "Things happen in order: first, next, then, last. Order helps us understand what happened in the past.": [1239.255, 6.344], "Here is a little flower's story.": [1246.049, 1.828], "First, a seed. Next, a small sprout. Last, a big flower.": [1248.327, 3.737], "See? First, next, last!": [1252.514, 1.542], "Put the storm night in order. Tap what happened FIRST, then the next one\u2026": [1254.506, 4.15], "First the wind blew, next the lamp went out, last the pages flew away.": [1259.106, 3.973], "What started the trouble?": [1263.529, 1.366], "First the wind blew. What did the wind do to the lamp?": [1265.345, 3.177], "Put my life in order, from first to last.": [1268.972, 2.394], "First a baby, next a kid, then a grown-up keeper. Last, Grandpa Tom today.": [1271.816, 4.941], "Think about a life in order: which comes next?": [1277.207, 2.539], "After a baby comes a kid. What comes after a kid?": [1280.196, 3.279], "First the storm came, then the light went out. Clever explorer!": [1283.925, 3.825], "A seed, a sprout and a flower. Which comes FIRST?": [1288.201, 2.984], "First the seed, next the sprout, last the flower.": [1291.635, 2.64], "What do we put in the ground?": [1294.725, 1.495], "A plant starts small.": [1296.67, 1.397], "The flower comes after the plant grows. What do you plant first?": [1298.517, 3.747], "A sprout grows out of something. What do you plant first?": [1302.715, 2.991], "the flower, the seed, or the sprout?": [1306.156, 1.847], "We learn about the past from historical sources.": [1308.453, 2.603], "Old photos, letters, diaries and maps are sources. Old objects are, too.": [1311.505, 4.781], "Let's open my grandma's memory box.": [1316.736, 2.205], "This old photo shows our bakery long, long ago. Look at the old oven!": [1319.391, 4.409], "The photo is a historical source. It tells us how the bakery looked back then.": [1324.25, 4.823], "Which one is a historical source?": [1329.523, 1.822], "An old letter was written long ago. So it can tell us about the past.": [1331.795, 4.195], "A source tells us about the past.": [1336.441, 1.873], "Which one was written long ago?": [1338.764, 1.686], "A new toy is from today. It cannot tell us about long ago.": [1340.9, 3.898], "You drew it today, so it is new. It is not from long ago.": [1345.248, 3.579], "an old letter, a brand-new toy, or a picture you drew today?": [1349.277, 3.321], "Grandma's old recipe card can tell us\u2026": [1353.049, 2.633], "It is an old recipe, so it tells us how people baked long ago.": [1356.132, 4.057], "The card is old. It is about the past.": [1360.639, 2.121], "A recipe tells how to bake something.": [1363.209, 2.079], "An old card cannot tell the future. It tells about the past.": [1365.739, 3.784], "Tomorrow has not happened yet. The card is about the past.": [1369.973, 3.608], "how people baked long ago, what the weather will be tomorrow, or what Grandma will cook tomorrow?": [1374.031, 5.447], "My memory box is full of sources, and now you can read them. History lives in old things! A muffin, {name}?": [1379.928, 6.187], "Which one can teach us about the past?": [1386.565, 2.077], "An old diary was written long ago, so it is a historical source.": [1389.091, 4.015], "It must be from long ago.": [1393.557, 1.599], "Someone wrote in it a long time ago.": [1395.606, 1.966], "An empty notebook has nothing in it. Nobody wrote in it long ago.": [1398.022, 4.115], "A toy from today is new. It cannot teach us about long ago.": [1402.587, 3.717], "an old diary, a new empty notebook, or a toy you got today?": [1406.754, 3.623], "Subtracting means taking away.": [1410.828, 1.825], "Here are 12 planks. The storm broke 5 of them. How many good planks are left?": [1413.103, 4.97], "A carpenter\u2019s trick: take away to get to 10 first!": [1418.523, 2.97], "12 \u2212 2 = 10. That takes away 2 of the 5.": [1421.943, 3.858], "Now take away the other 3: 10 \u2212 3 = 7. So 12 \u2212 5 = 7!": [1426.251, 6.157], "9 nails. Bramble uses 4. How many nails are left?": [1432.858, 3.355], "Count back 4 from 9: 8, 7, 6, 5. So 9 \u2212 4 = 5.": [1436.664, 5.577], "Start at 9 and count back 4.": [1442.691, 1.889], "9\u2026 8, 7\u2026 keep going until you count 4 numbers back.": [1445.03, 4.221], "6 is one too many. Start at 9 and count back 4.": [1449.702, 3.719], "4 is the nails he used. How many are LEFT after he uses them?": [1453.87, 4.243], "6, 5, or 4?": [1458.563, 1.443], "14 \u2212 4 = 10, and 10 \u2212 2 = 8. So 14 \u2212 6 = 8.": [1460.456, 6.476], "Take away 4 first to get to 10.": [1467.382, 1.961], "14 \u2212 4 = 10. Then take away 2 more.": [1469.793, 3.676], "9 is one too many. Take 4 to get to 10, then 2 more.": [1473.919, 3.881], "7 is one too few. Take 4 to get to 10, then take 2 more.": [1478.25, 4.263], "8, 9, or 7?": [1482.963, 1.399], "The roof needs 13 planks. Bramble has 8. How many more does he need?": [1484.812, 4.655], "8 and 5 more make 13, so 13 \u2212 8 = 5. He needs 5 more planks.": [1489.917, 6.429], "How far is it from 8 to 13?": [1496.797, 2.019], "8 + 2 = 10, and 3 more is 13.": [1499.265, 3.127], "4 is one too few. Count up from 8 to 13.": [1502.842, 3.332], "6 is one too many. Count up from 8 to 13.": [1506.624, 3.451], "4, 6, or 5?": [1510.525, 1.399], "7 good planks are left, so we cut 5 new ones for the bridge!": [1512.374, 3.899], "15 \u2212 5 = 10, and 10 \u2212 2 = 8.": [1516.722, 3.845], "Take away 5 first to get to 10.": [1521.017, 1.971], "15 \u2212 5 = 10. Then take away 2 more.": [1523.438, 3.763], "9 is one too many. Take 5 to get to 10, then 2 more.": [1527.651, 4.03], "7 is one too few. Take 5 to get to 10, then take 2 more.": [1532.131, 4.295], "9, 8, or 7?": [1536.876, 1.474], "A story problem asks a math question. Add or take away?": [1538.8, 3.861], "\"How many more?\" means compare: find the difference.": [1543.11, 2.693], "\"I baked 6 pies. Then I baked 3 more. How many pies now?\" More came: add. 6 + 3 = 9 pies.": [1546.253, 7.307], "\"Finn has 3 fish. I have 5. How many more do I have?\" Compare: 5 \u2212 3 = 2. I have 2 more.": [1554.01, 7.159], "Always say the answer with its word: 9 pies, 2 fish!": [1561.618, 3.536], "Maple had 11 muffins. She sold 4. How many muffins are left?": [1565.604, 4.723], "Sold means taking away: 11 \u2212 4 = 7. 7 muffins are left.": [1570.777, 5.291], "Did muffins come or go away?": [1576.518, 1.71], "Sold means they went away: 11 \u2212 4.": [1578.678, 2.852], "She SOLD muffins, so there are fewer, not more. Take away.": [1581.98, 3.426], "6 is one too few. Count back 4 from 11.": [1585.856, 3.332], "7 muffins, 15 muffins, or 6 muffins?": [1589.638, 2.987], "8 kids came for bread. Then 7 more kids came. How many kids came in all?": [1593.074, 4.966], "More came, so add: 8 + 7 = 15. 15 kids came.": [1598.49, 4.808], "More kids came: add.": [1603.748, 1.646], "8 needs 2 more to make 10. 10 and 5 more.": [1605.844, 3.137], "14 is one too few. 8 + 2 makes 10, and 5 more are left.": [1609.432, 4.714], "More kids CAME, so there are more. Add them together.": [1614.596, 3.066], "14 kids, 1 kid, or 15 kids?": [1618.112, 2.389], "Finn ate 5 cookies. Juniper ate 9 cookies. How many MORE did Juniper eat?": [1620.951, 5.201], "Compare: 9 \u2212 5 = 4. Juniper ate 4 more cookies.": [1626.602, 4.414], "\"How many more\" means compare: find the difference.": [1631.466, 2.563], "From 5 to 9: count up 6, 7, 8, 9.": [1634.479, 2.931], "14 is how many they ate together. The question asks how many MORE.": [1637.861, 4.309], "3 is one too few. Count up from 5 to 9.": [1642.619, 3.255], "14 cookies, 4 cookies, or 3 cookies?": [1646.325, 2.624], "Oh no! I had 12 pies and only 5 are left. 7 are missing\u2026 and there are cookie crumbs!": [1649.399, 6.517], "Nell saw 6 boats. Then 6 more came. How many boats now?": [1656.365, 4.002], "More came, so add: 6 + 6 = 12 boats.": [1660.817, 3.579], "More came: add.": [1664.846, 1.312], "6 + 6 is a double.": [1666.608, 1.764], "10 is too few. 6 and 6 more is a double.": [1668.822, 3.211], "More boats CAME, so there are more, not zero.": [1672.483, 2.363], "10 boats, 12 boats, or 0 boats?": [1675.296, 2.227], "Stories have three parts. The beginning tells who and where. The middle has a problem. The end solves it.": [1677.973, 6.557], "A character is a person or an animal in a story. A cat or a crab can be a character too!": [1684.981, 5.824], "Here\u2019s a story: \"Benny the mail dog lived at the post office.\" That\u2019s the beginning: who and where.": [1691.255, 5.446], "\"One windy day, a letter flew out of his bag!\" That\u2019s the middle: the problem.": [1697.151, 4.687], "\"He found it in the sand and brought it home.\" That\u2019s the end: the problem is solved.": [1702.288, 4.755], "\"Kit the cat lived on a boat.\" Which part of the story is this?": [1707.493, 3.692], "It tells who (Kit) and where (a boat): the beginning.": [1711.635, 2.587], "It tells who and where.": [1714.673, 1.356], "Stories start by meeting the characters.": [1716.479, 2.169], "The middle has the problem. This line just tells us who Kit is.": [1719.098, 3.769], "The end tells how things turn out. This line starts the story.": [1723.317, 3.857], "the middle, the beginning, or the end?": [1727.623, 1.869], "What is the problem in the middle of the story?": [1729.943, 2.289], "Her hat was gone: that\u2019s the problem in the middle.": [1732.681, 2.619], "What goes wrong?": [1735.75, 1.141], "\"One morning\u2026\"": [1737.341, 1.594], "That is the beginning, not a problem. What went wrong?": [1739.385, 3.163], "That is how it ends. What went wrong in the middle?": [1742.998, 2.765], "Kit\u2019s red hat was gone, Kit lived on a boat, or Kit put her hat back on?": [1746.213, 4.337], "How does the story end?": [1750.999, 1.463], "Kit finds her hat with the sleepy crab. She puts it back on, and the problem is solved.": [1752.912, 5.101], "How is the problem solved?": [1758.463, 1.643], "Look at the last sentence.": [1760.556, 1.579], "The story does not end that way. What did Kit want back?": [1762.585, 3.491], "That is not in the story. Think about what Kit was looking for.": [1766.526, 3.78], "Kit falls asleep, Kit gets her hat back, or The crab takes more hats?": [1770.756, 4.005], "Our island's story has a problem: the storm! You'll help write the end.": [1775.212, 4.015], "Which part of a story has the problem?": [1779.676, 2.085], "The problem is in the middle.": [1782.212, 1.593], "Think of Benny\u2019s letter.": [1784.255, 1.407], "Between the beginning and the end.": [1786.112, 1.741], "The end is where the problem gets solved. Where does it start?": [1788.304, 3.557], "The beginning tells who and where. The problem comes after that.": [1792.31, 3.593], "the end, the beginning, or the middle?": [1796.354, 1.838], "When big things happen, how does a character respond? How do they feel? What do they do? Look for word clues!": [1798.642, 6.555], "A feeling is how someone feels right now: happy, sad, scared.": [1805.647, 3.742], "A different story: the rain ruined Ben\u2019s sandcastle.": [1809.839, 3.192], "\"Ben felt upset.\" That\u2019s how he felt.": [1813.481, 2.483], "\"He built a new one, higher up the beach.\" That\u2019s what he did. He didn\u2019t give up!": [1816.414, 4.448], "How did Lulu feel when the sign fell?": [1821.312, 2.011], "The story says Lulu felt sad.": [1823.774, 1.976], "Find the feeling word in the story.": [1826.2, 1.913], "\"Lulu felt\u2026\"": [1828.563, 1.534], "Losing her sign was not happy news. Find the feeling word in the story.": [1830.547, 4.452], "Lulu was not tired. Find the feeling word in the story.": [1835.449, 3.569], "happy, sad, or sleepy?": [1839.468, 1.551], "What did Lulu do next?": [1841.469, 1.493], "She painted a new sign, even brighter than before.": [1843.411, 2.839], "Find what she did after she felt sad.": [1846.701, 2.266], "\"Then she got her brushes\u2026\"": [1849.417, 1.935], "Lulu did not go to sleep. She did something to fix the problem.": [1851.801, 4.041], "Lulu did not move away. She did something to fix the problem.": [1856.293, 3.941], "She painted a new sign, She went home to sleep, or She moved away?": [1860.683, 3.795], "How did her friends respond to the new sign?": [1864.929, 2.29], "Her friends cheered: they were happy for her.": [1867.669, 2.286], "Look at the end of the story.": [1870.405, 1.592], "\"Her friends\u2026\"": [1872.447, 1.603], "Crying is for sad news. The new sign was good news.": [1874.499, 3.35], "Her friends did not run away. They liked the new sign.": [1878.299, 3.16], "They cried, They ran away, or They cheered?": [1881.909, 2.115], "After the storm, our village didn't give up either. Everyone is fixing and helping!": [1884.475, 5.055], "A boy loses his kite but keeps looking until he finds it. How does he respond?": [1889.979, 4.782], "He keeps looking: he doesn\u2019t give up.": [1895.211, 2.007], "What does he do?": [1897.669, 1.139], "He keeps looking.": [1899.257, 1.187], "Nothing says he is sleepy. He keeps looking.": [1900.894, 2.805], "He does not forget. He keeps looking until he finds it.": [1904.149, 3.349], "He is sleepy, He doesn\u2019t give up, or He forgets about it?": [1907.948, 3.101], "Water is in oceans, rivers, lakes, ponds, even underground. Most is in oceans.": [1911.499, 5.647], "Rain is liquid: it flows. Ice is solid: it keeps its shape.": [1917.596, 3.769], "On my farm, water is everywhere! My pond, the river, and the well.": [1921.815, 4.051], "The well takes water from under the ground.": [1926.316, 2.212], "In winter, my pond freezes: the water turns solid. Ice!": [1928.978, 3.856], "Where is the biggest amount of water on Earth?": [1933.284, 2.414], "Most of Earth\u2019s water is in the oceans.": [1936.148, 2.179], "Think of the biggest water you know.": [1938.777, 1.795], "Oceans cover huge parts of Earth.": [1941.022, 1.971], "A puddle is tiny. Think of the biggest water on Earth.": [1943.444, 3.303], "Rivers have a lot of water. But the big salty water all over Earth has much, much more.": [1947.197, 5.456], "a puddle, the oceans, or the rivers?": [1953.103, 1.993], "Ice and snow are water that is\u2026": [1955.546, 2.143], "Ice and snow are solid water.": [1958.139, 1.869], "Can you hold ice in your hand?": [1960.458, 1.677], "Water that keeps its shape, like ice, is\u2026": [1962.586, 2.536], "Liquid water flows, like rain. Ice and snow keep their shape.": [1965.572, 3.751], "Steam floats in the air. Ice and snow are hard and keep their shape.": [1969.773, 3.902], "liquid, a gas, like steam, or solid?": [1974.125, 2.115], "Rosa\u2019s well gets water from\u2026": [1976.69, 2.092], "A well brings up water from under the ground.": [1979.232, 2.414], "Wells are deep holes.": [1982.096, 1.296], "There is water down in the ground.": [1983.842, 1.827], "Rain helps, but a well is a deep hole. Where does the well go?": [1986.119, 3.709], "Sea water is salty, and a well is dug deep down into the land. Where does its water hide?": [1990.277, 5.62], "under the ground, the clouds only, or the sea?": [1996.347, 2.363], "Now you know where water hides! My garden can grow again.": [1999.16, 3.417], "Which is solid water?": [2003.027, 1.463], "Snow is solid water.": [2004.94, 1.527], "Solid keeps its shape.": [2006.917, 1.375], "Which one is frozen?": [2008.741, 1.409], "Rain is liquid: it falls and flows. Solid water keeps its shape.": [2010.6, 4.329], "A river is liquid: it flows. Solid water keeps its shape.": [2015.378, 3.868], "rain, snow, or a river?": [2019.696, 1.474], "Earth changes. Quick changes: an earthquake, a volcano. Slow changes: waves wearing away rock over many years.": [2021.62, 7.677], "A source is where we find out about the past. A book or Grandpa\u2019s story can be a source!": [2029.748, 5.355], "Look at the rocks by the harbor. They have smooth holes.": [2035.552, 3.056], "Grandpa Tom says the holes were tiny when he was small. Now they\u2019re bigger. My book says waves did it slowly.": [2039.058, 6.931], "Two sources agree: waves wear away rock SLOWLY.": [2046.439, 3.141], "A volcano explodes. Is that a quick or a slow change?": [2050.03, 3.357], "A volcanic explosion is a quick change.": [2053.837, 2.407], "Does it take minutes or many years?": [2056.694, 1.922], "Volcanic explosions happen fast.": [2059.066, 2.097], "An explosion happens in a moment. That is fast.": [2061.612, 2.961], "slow, or quick?": [2065.024, 1.171], "Waves slowly wear smooth holes in the rocks. Quick or slow?": [2066.645, 3.548], "Waves wearing away rock is a slow change. It takes a very long time.": [2070.643, 4.381], "Does it happen in one day?": [2075.474, 1.548], "Water wears rock little by little.": [2077.472, 1.945], "The word \"slowly\" is in the question. Wearing away rock takes a long time.": [2079.867, 4.441], "How did Mo know the holes were made slowly?": [2084.758, 2.191], "Mo used two sources, Grandpa Tom and a book, as evidence.": [2087.398, 3.561], "Where did Mo get his information?": [2091.41, 1.795], "Grandpa Tom\u2026 and a book.": [2093.655, 1.727], "Mo did not guess. He checked with two sources.": [2095.832, 2.824], "One day is too short to see a slow change! Mo checked two sources.": [2099.106, 4.101], "He guessed, Two sources: Grandpa Tom and a book, or He watched the waves for one whole day?": [2103.658, 5.486], "One stormy night moved sand and rocks fast. Waves shape rocks slowly, too!": [2109.594, 4.909], "Which is a SLOW change?": [2114.952, 1.371], "Waves wear away rock slowly, over many years.": [2116.774, 2.703], "Which one takes many years?": [2119.926, 1.591], "Little by little\u2026": [2121.967, 1.516], "An earthquake lasts only a few seconds. That is fast.": [2123.933, 3.208], "A volcano explodes in a moment. That is fast.": [2127.591, 2.982], "the ground shaking in an earthquake, a volcano exploding, or waves wearing away rock?": [2131.023, 5.315], "A timeline puts events in order, from long ago to now. The earliest event goes first, on the left.": [2136.788, 6.345], "Let\u2019s put our island\u2019s story in order.": [2143.584, 2.127], "First, long ago, people built the lighthouse. Next, Keeper Rosalind chased the Crumb pirates away.": [2146.16, 6.165], "Last, not long ago, the big storm. Long ago \u2192 later \u2192 recently!": [2152.775, 5.421], "Put these in order, from long ago to recently.": [2158.645, 2.664], "First the lighthouse was built. Then Rosalind chased the pirates away. Not long ago came the big storm.": [2161.759, 7.009], "What happened longest ago?": [2169.218, 1.589], "The lighthouse came first; the storm was not long ago.": [2171.257, 3.209], "On a timeline, where does the oldest event go?": [2174.917, 2.676], "The oldest event goes at the beginning of the timeline.": [2178.043, 3.047], "Timelines start long ago.": [2181.539, 1.515], "Oldest first.": [2183.505, 1.141], "The end of a timeline is for the newest things.": [2185.096, 2.399], "The middle is for things in between. The oldest goes first.": [2187.945, 3.484], "at the end (right), at the beginning (left), or in the middle?": [2191.879, 3.084], "Which happened most recently?": [2195.413, 1.666], "The big storm was not long ago: the most recent.": [2197.529, 2.851], "Recently means not long ago.": [2200.83, 1.74], "Which one is closest to now?": [2203.02, 1.697], "The lighthouse was built long ago. What happened just now?": [2205.167, 3.396], "That was long, long ago. What happened just now?": [2209.013, 2.904], "people built the lighthouse, Rosalind chased the pirates, or the big storm?": [2212.367, 4.071], "Now let's add tonight to our timeline: the night we light the lighthouse again!": [2216.888, 4.224], "Which comes first on a timeline?": [2221.562, 1.913], "The event from longest ago comes first.": [2223.925, 2.297], "Timelines go from long ago to now.": [2226.673, 2.01], "This morning is very new, so it goes near the end.": [2229.133, 2.902], "Tomorrow has not happened yet. A timeline starts long ago.": [2232.485, 3.857], "what happened this morning, what happened longest ago, or what happens tomorrow?": [2236.791, 4.24]}}